o verify if k=3 is possible. Let's try to construct for general n.\n\nWe need to cover all points with n lines, exactly 3 sunny lines. So we have n-3 non-sunny lines. We can use vertical lines for columns with many points, and use sunny lines to cover the remaining points.\n\nWe need to see if we can always cover the last column (n) and second-last column (n-1) and third-last column (n-2) with 3 sunny lines. Let's examine the points in columns n, n-1, n-2:\n\n- Column n: (n,1)\n- Column n-1: (n-1,1), (n-1,2)\n- Column n-2: (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3)\n\nThat's total 1+2+3 = 6 points. We have 3 sunny lines, each can cover at most one point per column. So across the three columns, each sunny line can cover up to 3 points (one per column). So total coverage capacity is up to 9 points, but we need to cover 6 points. However we also have non-sunny lines (vertical/horizontal/diagonal) that can cover some of these points. But we need exactly 3 sunny lines, so we can allocate vertical lines for some columns, but we need to ensure we have exactly n-3 non-sunny lines. Typically we would use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3 (i.e., n-3 vertical lines). Then we have columns n-2, n-1, n left uncovered. We have 3 sunny lines to cover those columns. Since each sunny line can intersect each column at most once, we can assign each sunny line to cover one point in each of the three columns. So we need to pick three points, one from each column, such that the three points are not collinear with slope 0, infinite, or -1 (i.e., the line connecting them is not vertical/horizontal/diagonal). Actually each sunny line is a line, not a point. We need to assign each sunny line to cover a set of points across columns. A sunny line can intersect each column at at most one point, but it can intersect multiple columns. So a sunny line can cover up to three points (one per column). So we could try to cover all six points using three sunny lines, each covering two points (maybe one per column). But we need to ensure each line is not vertical/horizontal/diagonal.\n\nLet's try to find three sunny lines that cover all six points. For example, we could try to pair points across columns: line L1 passes through (n-2,1) and (n-1,2) and (n,3?) but (n,3) is not a point because column n only has b=1. So cannot. Let's think systematically.\n\nWe have columns:\n\n- Column n: only point (n,1)\n- Column n-1: points (n-1,1), (n-1,2)\n- Column n-2: points (n-2,1), (n-2,2), (n-2,3)\n\nWe need to cover these points with 3 sunny lines. Since column n has only one point, any line covering it must pass through (n,1). So each sunny line that covers (n,1) will also intersect columns n-1 and n-2 at some points (maybe). So we could have one sunny line L1 passing through (n,1) and also intersect column n-1 at some point (n-1,b1) and column n-2 at some point (n-2,b2). That line must not be vertical/horizontal/diagonal. Since it's not vertical (different x), not horizontal (different y), not slope -1 (we need to check). So we can choose b1 and b2 such that the line through (n,1) and (n-1,b1) has slope (b1-1)/(n-1 - n) = (b1-1)/(-1) = 1 - b1. So slope = 1 - b1. For the line to also pass through (n-2,b2), we need slope = (b2 - b1)/(n-2 - (n-1)) = (b2 - b1)/(-1) = b1 - b2. So we need 1 - b1 = b1 - b2 => b2 = 2b1 - 1. So we need integer b1, b2 such that b1 ∈ {1,2} (since column n-1 has b=1,2) and b2 ∈ {1,2,3} (since column n-2 has b=1,2,3). Let's try b1=1 => b2 = 2*1 - 1 = 1. So line through (n,1) and (n-1,1) is horizontal (y=1), slope 0, not sunny. So not allowed.\n\nb1=2 => b2 = 2*2 - 1 = 3. So line through (n,1) and (n-1,2) has slope = (2-1)/(-1) = -1, which is slope -1, not sunny. So not allowed.\n\nThus any line through (n,1) and a point in column n-1 will be either horizontal (if b1=1) or slope -1 (if b1=2). Both are not sunny. So we cannot have a sunny line that passes through (n,1) and any point in column n-1. So any sunny line covering (n,1) cannot intersect column n-1 at any of its points. It could intersect column n-1 at a point not in the set of required points (i.e., a point with b > 2). But that point is not required to be covered, but the line would intersect column n-1 at some point (n-1,b) where b is not necessarily integer or not required. That's okay; the line can intersect column n-1 at any point, but it doesn't need to cover a required point there. So we can have a sunny line that passes through (n,1) and some other point not in the required set, e.g., (n-1,0) (but b must be positive integer? Actually points are only required for positive integer a,b. The line can intersect at any real point, not necessarily integer. So we can choose a line through (n,1) with slope not 0, -1, infinite, e.g., slope 2: line y-1 = 2(x-n). That line passes through (n,1) and (n-1, -1). That's okay. It doesn't intersect any required point in column n-1. So we can have a sunny line covering (n,1) only.\n\nThus we can have one sunny line covering (n,1) only. Then we need to cover the points in columns n-1 and n-2 with the remaining two sunny lines. Let's see if we can cover all points in columns n-1 and n-2 with two sunny lines.\n\nColumn n-1 has points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Column n-2 has points (n-2,1),(n-2,2),(n-2,3). So total 5 points. Two sunny lines can intersect each column at most one point each. So across columns n-1 and n-2, each sunny line can cover at most 2 points (one per column). So two sunny lines can cover at most 4 points. But we have 5 points. So we need to use non-sunny lines to cover some of them. But we have used all n-3 vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3. So we have no non-sunny lines left (since we have exactly n-3 non-sunny lines, all used as vertical lines). However we could allocate some of the non-sunny lines to be horizontal or diagonal instead of vertical, to cover some points in columns n-1 and n-2. But we need exactly n-3 non-sunny lines. We can choose which columns to cover with vertical lines. Perhaps we don't need to cover all columns 1,...,n-3 with vertical lines; we could use some horizontal/diagonal lines to cover some points in columns n-1 and n-2, reducing the load on sunny lines.\n\nThus we need to find a construction for any n≥3 using exactly 3 sunny lines and n-3 non-sunny lines (vertical/horizontal/diagonal) that covers all points. The solution says \"Use 3 sunny lines (construction similar to k=2). ✓\". But they didn't provide details. So we need to verify if k=3 is indeed possible.\n\nLet's try to construct for general n.\n\nOne approach: Use vertical lines for columns 1,...,n-3. That's n-3 vertical lines. Then we have columns n-2, n-1, n left. We have 3 sunny lines to cover all points in these three columns. As we saw, column n has 1 point, column n-1 has 2 points, column n-2 has 3 points. That's 6 points. We have 3 sunny lines, each can intersect each column at most once, so total capacity is 3*3 = 9 points, which is enough. But we need to assign points to lines such that each line covers at most one point per column, and each point is covered by some line. Since we have 3 lines, we can assign each line to cover up to 3 points (one per column). So we need to partition the 6 points into at most 3 groups, each group containing at most one point from each column. That's possible: we can assign each line to cover two points (maybe one from column n-2 and one from column n-1, etc.) Let's try to assign:\n\n- L1: cover (n,1) and (n-2,1) maybe? But line through (n,1) and (n-2,1) is horizontal (y=1), not sunny. So cannot.\n\n- L1: cover (n,1) and (n-1,1). That's horizontal, not sunny.\n\n- L1: cover (n,1) and (n-1,2). That's slope -1, not sunny.\n\nThus any line through (n,1) and any point in column n-1 is not sunny. So L1 cannot cover any point in column n-1. It can only cover (n,1) and maybe a point in column n-2 (if we choose a point such that the line is not horizontal or slope -1). Let's see: line through (n,1) and (n-2,b2) has slope (b2-1)/(n-2 - n) = (b2-1)/(-2) = (1-b