line can intersect each column at most once, and there are 3 columns). So total capacity is 3*3 = 9 points, enough. But we need to assign points to lines.\n\nWe can try to assign each line to cover one point in each column. For column 1 (a=1), we have points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3). For column 2, points (2,1),(2,2). For column 3, point (3,1). So we need to assign each line a point in column 1, column 2, column 3. Since there are 3 lines, we can assign each line a distinct point in column 1 (so each line gets one of (1,1),(1,2),(1,3)). For column 2, we have only 2 points, so two of the lines will get a point in column 2, the third line will not intersect column 2 at a required point. For column 3, only one point, so one line will get (3,1). So we need to find lines that pass through these assigned points.\n\nLet's try to assign:\n\n- L1: (1,1) and (2,2) and (3,1)? Check if these three points are collinear. Compute slope between (1,1) and (2,2): slope = (2-1)/(2-1) = 1. Check if (3,1) lies on line y = x? At x=3, y=3, not 1. So not collinear.\n\n- L1: (1,1) and (2,1) and (3,1) are collinear horizontally (y=1), not sunny.\n\n- L1: (1,1) and (2,2) and (3,1) not collinear.\n\n- L1: (1,1) and (2,2) define line y = x. That's slope 1, sunny. It passes through (3,3) which is not a required point. So L1 covers (1,1) and (2,2). Good.\n\n- L2: (1,2) and (2,1) define line slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, which is slope -1, not sunny. So cannot use that.\n\n- L2: (1,2) and (2,2) define horizontal line y=2, not sunny.\n\n- L2: (1,2) and (2,1) not allowed. (1,2) and (3,1) slope = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2, allowed. So L2 can be line through (1,2) and (3,1). That line also passes through (2,1.5) not integer. So L2 covers (1,2) and (3,1). Good.\n\n- L3: (1,3) and (2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed. So L3 covers (1,3) and (2,1). So we have covered all points: (1,1) and (2,2) by L1; (1,2) and (3,1) by L2; (1,3) and (2,1) by L3. So all 6 points are covered. All lines have slopes 1, -1/2, -2, none of which are 0, infinite, -1. So all lines are sunny. So k=3 works for n=3.\n\nThus k=3 is possible for n=3.\n\nNow for n=4, can we have k=4 (all lines sunny)? Let's try to see if we can cover all points with 4 sunny lines. Points: (1,1)-(1,4), (2,1)-(2,3), (3,1)-(3,2), (4,1). That's 10 points. We have 4 lines, each can intersect each column at most once, so each line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So total capacity is 4*4 = 16, enough. But we need to assign points to lines such that each line passes through at most one point per column, and each point is covered.\n\nWe can try to assign each line a point in each column, but column 4 has only one point, so only one line will cover it. Column 3 has 2 points, so two lines will cover them. Column 2 has 3 points, so three lines will cover them. Column 1 has 4 points, so all four lines will cover them. So we need to assign points accordingly.\n\nWe need to find 4 lines L1,...,L4 such that:\n\n- In column 1 (x=1), assign points (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4) to L1,L2,L3,L4 respectively.\n- In column 2 (x=2), assign points (2,1),(2,2),(2,3) to three of the lines, leaving one line not covering a required point in column 2.\n- In column 3 (x=3), assign points (3,1),(3,2) to two lines.\n- In column 4 (x=4), assign point (4,1) to one line.\n\nWe need to find lines that pass through these assigned points.\n\nWe can try to assign:\n\n- L1: (1,1), (2,2), (3,3?), (4,4?) but (3,3) not a required point (max b=2 for column 3). So L1 cannot pass through (3,3). So we need to assign L1 to (1,1), (2,2), (3,? maybe 2?), (4,1?). Let's try L1: (1,1) and (2,2) define line y = x. At x=3, y=3 (not required). At x=4, y=4 (not required). So L1 covers only (1,1) and (2,2). That's okay.\n\n- L2: (1,2) and (2,1) define line slope = (1-2)/(2-1) = -1, not sunny. So not allowed.\n\n- L2: (1,2) and (2,3) define line slope = (3-2)/(2-1) = 1, sunny. At x=3, y = 2 + (3-1)*1 = 4? Actually line through (1,2) slope 1: y = x + 1. At x=3, y=4 (not required).