lowed. So not.\n\nThus covering the last two columns with two sunny lines seems impossible. But maybe we can replace a different set of columns with sunny lines, not just the last two. For instance, we could replace column n-2 (which has 3 points) with a sunny line covering two of its points, and use another sunny line to cover the third point plus some other point from another column. But we need to ensure we still have n lines total.\n\nLet's think more systematically. We have n columns, each column i (x=i) has points (i,b) for b=1,...,n+1-i. So column i has n+1-i points. The total number of points is sum_{i=1}^{n} (n+1-i) = n(n+1)/2.\n\nWe have n lines. If we use vertical lines for all columns, we have n lines covering all points. That's k=0. If we want to use some sunny lines, we need to replace some vertical lines with sunny lines that can cover points from multiple columns (or multiple points within a column). Since we have exactly n lines, we cannot have more than n lines. So we need to cover all points with n lines, exactly k sunny.\n\nThus the problem reduces to: For each k, can we find a set of n lines, exactly k of which are sunny, covering all points? Equivalent to: can we cover the points with n lines, using at most k sunny lines? Actually exactly k, but we can always add extra non-sunny lines that are redundant? But they must be distinct lines. So we cannot have duplicate lines. So we need exactly n distinct lines.\n\nThus we need to find a covering of the points with n lines, exactly k sunny.\n\nWe need to find all possible k.\n\nWe have found constructions for k=0,1,3. We need to see if k=2 is impossible, and if any k≥4 is possible.\n\nThe solution says k≥4 is impossible. Let's examine that.\n\nIf we have k≥4 sunny lines, we have at most n-4 non-sunny lines. Non-sunny lines can be vertical, horizontal, or diagonal. The vertical lines can cover each column. But we have only n-4 vertical lines (or other non-sunny lines). So we cannot cover all n column